  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe potential for strong to severe thunderstorms for Tuesday \nafternoon for the entire BGM CWA. The primary threat is wind with \nlarge hail and an isolated tornado or two can't be ruled out. There \nis a 'Slight Risk' across the entire area from the Storm Prediction \nCenter. An 'Enhanced Risk'is in place across the Wyoming Valley \narea, along with areas further south into Pennsylvania. This will \nall unfold as a strong trough drops out of Canada and tracks across \nthe Great Lakes, while at the same time a sub 995 mb sfc low will \ntrack near the southern tip of James Bay, dragging its associated \nfrontal boundaries across the region. \n\nSome morning showers and scattered thunderstorms will be possible \nfor Tuesday as the warm front associated with the system tracks \nnortheast. Though primary timing for the strong to severe \nthunderstorm potential is expected for the afternoon into \nevening hours. This is when the warm sector of the system will \nincrease instability over the region, while earlier shear values\nwill increase some toward 40 kts. Instability will increase to \naround 1,500 J/kg for SB CAPE and closer to 2,000 J/kg for MU \nCAPE. A pre-frontal trough tracking across the area during the \nafternoon into the early evening will provide the forcing for \nthe storms to develop (if not already developed upstream). \nStorms should move pretty quick with the overall flow in place, \nbut heavy downpours can be expected at times with the \nthunderstorms as PWat values increase to the 1.75-2.00\" range. \nIf a few thunderstorms hit the same area in a short window of \ntime, then the potential for localized flooding issues will \nincrease. A 'Marginal Risk' for excessive rainfall is in place \nfor the entire area on Tuesday from WPC. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe best chance for showers will occur as the cold front associated \nwith the system tracks through the region during the early morning \nhours. After, some lingering showers will be possible as the axis of \nthe large trough over the region tracks across and then east of the \narea by later Wednesday. \n\nRidging will increase across the region as a sfc highs slide east \nfrom the mid Mississippi Valley to the coast of New England from \nThursday morning through Saturday morning. \n\nCooler temperatures for Wednesday and Thursday will start to warm \nlater into the week and the weekend as return flow increases behind \nthe sfc high shifting east.",
